# Store text files with LF in the repository AND check them out with LF on every platform,
# including Windows.
#
# The "eol=lf" half matters more than it looks. With plain "text=auto", git converts to the
# platform's native ending on checkout, so the same file is CRLF on a Windows machine and LF on a
# Linux CI runner. Any tool that has an opinion about line endings then disagrees with itself
# depending on where it runs. .editorconfig is exactly such a tool, and the resulting IDE0055
# build failure appears only in CI, which is the worst place to debug it.
#
# One authority for line endings: this file. .editorconfig matches it rather than restating it.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Batch files are the real exception: the Windows command interpreter can mis-handle LF.
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.bat   text eol=crlf
# Visual Studio rewrites .sln files with CRLF, so fighting it just creates noisy diffs.
*.sln   text eol=crlf

# Give git a language-aware diff for C#, so hunk headers show the enclosing member.
*.cs    text diff=csharp

# Never normalize or diff these as text.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.snk   binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
